From: Michael Phan <sonunix@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 13 Aug 2026 23:50:00 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath11k: use kmalloc-backed skbs for rx ring buffers

dev_alloc_skb() serves sub-page allocations from the shared per-CPU
page_frag cache: 32KB compound pages filled append-only and freed only
when every skb carved from them is gone. ath11k posts long-lived 2KB
buffers to its DMA rings (rx refill, rx mon status, CE rx) from this
cache, interleaved with short-lived allocations. Every ring buffer
recycle lands in a new frag chunk, so each surviving ring buffer pins
up to 32KB. Under steady PPDU/event churn (an AP with associated
stations is enough, no data traffic required) the pinned set grows
toward ring_entries * 32KB per ring, hundreds of MB in the worst case.

On Linksys MX4200 (IPQ8074, 512MB) this manifested as MemAvailable
draining at 80-490MB/day (bursting to 400MB/h), invisible to slabinfo
and vmstat, fully recovered by reloading ath11k. page_owner attributed
the growth to order-3 __netdev_alloc_skb chunks owned by the mon status
replenish and CE rx post paths (1112 and 802 chunks after one 35MB burn
window).

Allocate the ring buffers with alloc_skb() instead: kmalloc-backed heads
are freed independently and the slab allocator refills partial slabs, so
pinning cannot compound. Layout is kept identical to dev_alloc_skb() by
reserving NET_SKB_PAD explicitly.

Validated on a 9-node MX4200 fleet: MemAvailable flat (-2 to -6 MB/day,
within noise) over 54h with zero driver-reload interventions, versus
80-490 MB/day and eventual OOM before the change.

Signed-off-by: Michael Phan <sonunix@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/ce.c    | 3 ++-
 drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/dp_rx.c | 9 ++++++---
 2 files changed, 8 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/dp_rx.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/dp_rx.c
@@ -371,11 +371,13 @@ int ath11k_dp_rxbufs_replenish(struct at
 	num_remain = req_entries;
 
 	while (num_remain > 0) {
-		skb = dev_alloc_skb(DP_RX_BUFFER_SIZE +
-				    DP_RX_BUFFER_ALIGN_SIZE);
+		skb = alloc_skb(DP_RX_BUFFER_SIZE +
+				DP_RX_BUFFER_ALIGN_SIZE +
+				NET_SKB_PAD, GFP_ATOMIC);
 		if (!skb)
 			break;
 
+		skb_reserve(skb, NET_SKB_PAD);
 		if (!IS_ALIGNED((unsigned long)skb->data,
 				DP_RX_BUFFER_ALIGN_SIZE)) {
 			skb_pull(skb,
@@ -2839,12 +2841,14 @@ static struct sk_buff *ath11k_dp_rx_allo
 	struct sk_buff *skb;
 	dma_addr_t paddr;
 
-	skb = dev_alloc_skb(DP_RX_BUFFER_SIZE +
-			    DP_RX_BUFFER_ALIGN_SIZE);
+	skb = alloc_skb(DP_RX_BUFFER_SIZE +
+			DP_RX_BUFFER_ALIGN_SIZE +
+			NET_SKB_PAD, GFP_ATOMIC);
 
 	if (!skb)
 		goto fail_alloc_skb;
 
+	skb_reserve(skb, NET_SKB_PAD);
 	if (!IS_ALIGNED((unsigned long)skb->data,
 			DP_RX_BUFFER_ALIGN_SIZE)) {
 		skb_pull(skb, PTR_ALIGN(skb->data, DP_RX_BUFFER_ALIGN_SIZE) -
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/ce.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/ce.c
@@ -331,12 +331,13 @@ static int ath11k_ce_rx_post_pipe(struct
 
 	spin_lock_bh(&ab->ce.ce_lock);
 	while (pipe->rx_buf_needed) {
-		skb = dev_alloc_skb(pipe->buf_sz);
+		skb = alloc_skb(pipe->buf_sz + NET_SKB_PAD, GFP_ATOMIC);
 		if (!skb) {
 			ret = -ENOMEM;
 			goto exit;
 		}
 
+		skb_reserve(skb, NET_SKB_PAD);
 		WARN_ON_ONCE(!IS_ALIGNED((unsigned long)skb->data, 4));
 
 		paddr = dma_map_single(ab->dev, skb->data,
